  small footer that exports to [38;5;153mmodule.exports[39m under Node and hangs [38;5;153mwindow.AppLog[39m in the browser. Two sinks plug in
  behind one interface: Node appends NDJSON to a rotating file, browser writes to a capped IndexedDB ring buffer and
  offers [38;5;153mexportLogs()[39m. [38;5;153mindex.html[39m gains two [38;5;153m<script>[39m tags before [38;5;153mapp.js[39m. Buys you a single copy of the safety-critical
  code with zero dependencies and zero tooling. Costs a slightly dated dual-export shim and a browser global. Easy to
  change later — swapping to modules or adding a network sink touches one file each.

  [1mB. ESM everywhere.[0m Write the core once as a real ES module; browser uses [38;5;153m<script type="module">[39m, Node imports it
  natively. Cleanest end state and no interop shim. But it requires converting [38;5;153msrc/index.js[39m and [38;5;153msrc/utils.js[39m off
  CommonJS and restructuring [38;5;153mapp.js[39m into a module — changes to working code that have nothing to do with logging, which
  makes the logging change harder to review and harder to revert. Also hard to undo once the rest of the repo has moved.

  [1mC. Common schema, two independent implementations.[0m No shared file at all: agree on a record format, write a small
  idiomatic logger on each side. Simplest possible per half and no module puzzle. The cost is the one I'd refuse: the
  allow-list lives in two places, so the two copies drift and a field that's redacted in Node quietly isn't in the
  browser. Wins only if the two halves were expected to diverge, which isn't the case here.

  [3mDiscarded: pulling in pino + loglevel. Two dependencies, two different APIs to bridge anyway, and neither ships [0m
  [3mallow-list redaction or IndexedDB persistence — you'd write the parts that matter yourself regardless.[0m

  [1mMy recommendation: A.[0m It's the only one that keeps redaction in a single place without dragging unrelated files into
  the change, and every expensive thing it defers (ESM migration, a network sink) stays cheap to do later.
